---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Contact from "../components/Contact.astro";
import { SITE, href } from "../lib/catalog.js";
const UPDATED = "23 November 2026";
---
<Base title="Privacy policy" description="What Quitanda collects, why, who handles it and how to remove it, including the small Google ads that help pay for the site.">
  <main class="wrap page narrow">
    <article class="prose">
      <header>
        <time>Last updated {UPDATED}</time>
        <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
      </header>

      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You can browse and download everything without an account.</li>
        <li>The site shows a few small ads from Google to help pay for itself. Google may use cookies to choose them. In the EU, the UK and Switzerland you’re asked first, and you can say no. <a href="#ads">How the ads work</a>.</li>
        <li>No analytics, and Quitanda itself sets no cookies.</li>
        <li>Your account data is never sold, rented or shared with advertisers.</li>
        <li>If you sign in, we keep your name, email address and profile picture so your account works.</li>
        <li>The newsletter is opt-in. It uses your name and email address to send you news and for nothing else.</li>
        <li>You can download everything we hold about you, unsubscribe, or delete your account yourself, at any time, from <a href={href("/account/")}>your account page</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
      <p>Quitanda is a personal project, not a business. The ads help cover its costs. It is run by <a href="https://github.com/mrbarkan">mrbarkan</a>, who decides how the data described here is used (the “controller”, in the law’s words). You can reach them at <strong class="selectable">{SITE.contactEmail}</strong>.</p>

      <h2>What we collect, and why</h2>

      <h3>When you only browse</h3>
      <p>Quitanda itself stores nothing about you. Your browser remembers two preferences on your own device and never sends them to us: the iPod you picked in the “My iPod” menu, and whether you hid the “Fresh in” panel.</p>
      <p>Like any website, the companies that deliver the pages see your IP address and browser type when your browser asks for them. See <a href="#who">who handles your data</a>.</p>
      <p>The ads on the page come from Google, which receives the same kind of information and may use cookies. That is described next.</p>

      <h3 id="ads">Ads and cookies</h3>
      <p>Quitanda shows a small number of ads served by Google AdSense. The money helps cover the domain and running costs. Quitanda doesn’t choose the individual ads and never learns who saw or clicked which one.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Google and its partners use cookies and similar technology to show ads, limit how often you see the same one, measure them and fight fraud.</li>
        <li>With your permission, Google may also personalize ads using your visits to this site and to other sites. Google’s use of advertising cookies lets it and its partners serve ads based on those visits.</li>
        <li>In the EU, the UK and Switzerland, a consent message from Google asks you first. Personalized ads and the cookies they need are only used if you agree. If you say no, you may still see ads, chosen without personal profiling. You can change your answer at any time with the “Privacy and cookie settings” link on the page.</li>
        <li>Anywhere, you can turn off personalized ads in <a href="https://myadcenter.google.com/">Google’s My Ad Center</a>, and opt out of other companies’ personalized ads at <a href="https://www.aboutads.info/choices/">aboutads.info</a> (or <a href="https://www.youronlinechoices.eu/">youronlinechoices.eu</a> in Europe).</li>
        <li>An ad blocker also works. The site doesn’t need the ads to function, and nothing is locked behind them.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Google explains what it does with this information in <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites">How Google uses information from sites that use its services</a>. The legal basis for personalized ads and advertising cookies in Europe is your consent.</p>

      <h3>When you sign in</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="plain-table">
          <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Why</th><th>Who can see it</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Name, email address, profile picture and account ID from the Google account you sign in with</th><td>To create your account and recognize you when you return</td><td>Only you and the site’s admin</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Public profile: display name, picture, and an optional short bio and link</th><td>To show who shared a theme</td><td>Everyone</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Whether you’re an iPod owner or a maker</th><td>To show you the right guide</td><td>Only you and the site’s admin</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">The iPods you list on your account page: each one’s model and an optional nickname</th><td>So you can say which of your iPods a report is for</td><td>Only you and the site’s admin</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Your email address is never shown on the site. The legal basis is that we need this information to provide the account you asked for.</p>

      <h3>When you subscribe to the newsletter</h3>
      <p>Only if you tick the box: your name, the email address you signed in with, and the date you agreed. They are used to send you occasional Quitanda news and updates, and for nothing else. The legal basis is your consent, which you can withdraw at any time by unsubscribing on your account page or from any email. Unsubscribing deletes the record.</p>

      <h3>When you share something</h3>
      <p>Themes you upload, with their screenshots and descriptions, are public once approved, alongside your display name. If you report a problem with a theme, your report is kept so the admin can act on it.</p>
      <p>If you say whether a firmware or app works on your iPod, that answer is stored with your account ID, the model, and which of your listed iPods it was for (by an ID, never its nickname), and counted with everyone else’s. The site shows only the totals, never who said what.</p>

      <h2>What we don’t do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We don’t sell or rent personal data, and we don’t give account data (your name, email address, profile or anything you share) to advertisers or data brokers.</li>
        <li>We don’t run analytics, and Quitanda doesn’t build profiles of visitors. Google’s ad service works as described under <a href="#ads">ads and cookies</a>.</li>
        <li>We don’t make automated decisions about you.</li>
        <li>We don’t ask for more than is listed above, and we don’t read your Google account beyond the name, email address and picture it shares at sign-in.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="who">Who handles your data</h2>
      <p>Quitanda runs on services from two companies:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Google (Firebase)</strong> provides sign-in and stores accounts, profiles, the newsletter list and shared themes. Google’s servers also deliver the site’s fonts.</li>
        <li><strong>GitHub</strong> hosts the site’s pages.</li>
        <li><strong>Google (AdSense)</strong> serves the ads, as described under <a href="#ads">ads and cookies</a>. For personalized ads, Google acts on its own account rather than on Quitanda’s behalf, under <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google’s privacy policy</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Both are based in the United States, so your data is stored and processed there. Both commit to the safeguards European law requires for such transfers.</p>
      <p>The newsletter hasn’t started yet. When it does, it will be sent through an email delivery service, which will receive subscribers’ names and addresses for that purpose only. This page will name the service before the first email goes out.</p>
      <p>We’ll disclose data if the law requires it, and not otherwise.</p>

      <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Account and profile:</strong> until you delete your account.</li>
        <li><strong>Newsletter record:</strong> until you unsubscribe.</li>
        <li><strong>Shared themes:</strong> until you delete them or your account, or the admin removes them.</li>
        <li><strong>Advertising cookies:</strong> they’re set and kept by Google on its own schedule. You can delete them from your browser at any time.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Deleting your account removes your profile, your settings, the iPods you listed, your newsletter record, the themes you shared with their files, the reports you sent, and the sign-in itself. Copies in our providers’ routine backups expire on their schedule.</p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>You can see, correct, download and delete your data yourself. <a href={href("/gdpr/")}>Your data rights</a> explains each right and exactly how to use it here.</p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>Quitanda isn’t aimed at children. Please don’t create an account if you’re under 16.</p>

      <h2>Security</h2>
      <p>Sign-in is handled by Google, so Quitanda never sees or stores a password. Access rules limit who can read each record, and all connections are encrypted. No system is perfect; if a breach ever affects your data, you’ll be told.</p>

      <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>If this policy changes in a way that matters, it will be announced on the <a href={href("/blog/")}>blog</a> before it takes effect, and newsletter subscribers will be told by email. The date at the top shows the latest version.</p>

      <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
      <Contact>use <a href={href("/account/")}>your account page</a> to download or delete your data and to unsubscribe.</Contact>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
